--TEST--
`[]` works only on an array, so reading `$map[$k]` or writing `$map[$k] = $v` on a `Core\ObjectMap` does not compile, and `get` and `set` do the same work — `rule:iteration/keyed-interfaces`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:iteration/keys-and-values-from-a-map
final class Tag {
    public string $name;

    public function constructor(string $name) {
        $this->name = $name;
    }
}

Tag $red = new Tag("red");
Core\ObjectMap<Tag, int> $weights = new Core\ObjectMap<Tag, int>();

// A map has no `[]`. Reading with it does not compile.
var $n = $weights[$red];

// Writing with `[]` does not compile either.
$weights[$red] = 3;

// `set` and `get` are the methods that write and read an entry. These compile.
$weights->set($red, 3);
echo $weights->get($red) ?? 0, "\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0482]: `Core\ObjectMap<Tag, int>` cannot be subscripted
   --> case.nvs:15:10
%A
error[E0482]: `Core\ObjectMap<Tag, int>` cannot be subscripted
   --> case.nvs:18:1
%A
